        <div id="speed-control" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Speed Controls: Single Speed, Dials and Pace-Sensing Handles
          </h2>

          <p>
            The second thing that decides whether you enjoy a self-propelled mower is how you set its speed. Budget models often have a single fixed speed. Mid-range models add a dial or lever on the handle. The more refined systems, such as Toro's Personal Pace and similar pressure-sensing bars, read how hard you push and adjust automatically.
          </p>

          <p>
            A single fixed speed sounds limiting, and it is, but it is not automatically bad. If you walk at a steady clip and your lot is open, a fixed speed just becomes the pace you mow at. Problems start when the fixed speed does not match you. Taller owners often find budget drives too slow; shorter or older owners often find them pulling ahead. Ask to feel the drive engage in the store before you buy, or check owner reviews that mention speed specifically.
          </p>

          <p>
            Variable-speed dials let you pick a pace and keep it, and they are useful for slowing down in thick grass so the blade has time to cut and the bag fills evenly. Pace-sensing handles go further: you just walk, and the mower follows. That makes them excellent around obstacles, because the mower slows when you slow without a hand needing to move. The trade-off is one more mechanism to maintain, though owners rarely report trouble with it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Battery mowers handle speed electronically, which tends to feel smooth and lets some models offer a wider range than belt-driven gas drives. Whatever the system, a slower setting is your friend in tall growth. Rushing a self-propelled mower through overgrown grass is the most common reason for a ragged cut, a clogged chute and a drained battery.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="gas-or-battery" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Gas or Battery: Where Each One Wins
          </h2>

          <p>
            Battery self-propelled mowers have improved to the point where they are the better choice for many suburban lots. A modern 21-inch battery mower with a strong pack and a brushless motor cuts well, starts instantly, is far quieter and asks for almost no routine maintenance. There is no oil to change, no carburetor to gum up over winter and no fuel can sitting in the garage.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gas still wins on two fronts: runtime and sustained heavy load. A tank of fuel will mow a large yard in one go, and a gas engine does not fade as it works through thick, wet grass. Battery runtime is quoted on the box under good conditions; in long damp spring growth it can fall well short of that. If your lawn is over roughly half an acre, or you regularly let grass get long between mows, gas or a battery mower with two packs is the realistic answer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cost of ownership is closer than the sticker suggests. A gas mower needs oil, a spark plug, an air filter and fuel stabilizer every year, plus the occasional carburetor cleaning when fuel sits too long. A battery mower needs none of that, but its battery is a wear item that will eventually lose capacity, and a replacement high-capacity pack can cost a large share of the mower price. If you already own batteries from the same tool platform, the battery case gets stronger.
          </p>

          <p>
            Noise and fumes are the quality-of-life factors. Battery mowers are quiet enough to mow early on a weekend without annoying neighbors, and there is no exhaust to breathe. For anyone who mows close to a patio or house windows, that difference is noticeable every single week. If the yard fits a battery mower's runtime, it is usually the more pleasant tool to own.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">Quick rule</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Under a third of an acre and mostly flat? Battery is usually the better buy.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Above half an acre, on steep ground, or if you mow tall wet grass often, a rear-drive gas mower or a battery mower with a second pack avoids mid-mow stops.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="yard-size" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Matching the Mower to Your Yard Size
          </h2>

          <p>
            Lot size tells you less than mowable area. A half-acre lot with a large house, driveway, patio and planting beds may only have a quarter acre of actual grass. Pace it out or use a satellite map tool to estimate the lawn itself. That number, not the deed, is what you should compare against battery runtime ratings and deck width.
          </p>

          <p>
            For lawns up to about a quarter acre of grass, a 21-inch self-propelled of any type will handle the job comfortably in 30 to 45 minutes. This is where battery models shine and where a smaller, lighter machine is more pleasant than a heavy one. If the yard is truly tiny, consider whether you need self-propulsion at all; our guide to the <a href="/best-lawn-mower-for-small-yard/">best mowers for small yards</a> covers push, reel and corded options that cost less.
          </p>

          <p>
            Between a quarter and half an acre, a strong 21- or 22-inch self-propelled is still the right tool, but drive type and runtime start to matter more. Gas models finish without stopping; battery models need either a large pack or a spare. Expect 45 minutes to an hour and a quarter depending on obstacles.
          </p>

          <p>
            Past half an acre, walking gets long. A 30-inch walk-behind trims the time noticeably, and it fits through gates a rider cannot. Once you are past three quarters of an acre of open grass, though, a riding mower usually makes more sense. Our <a href="/best-riding-lawn-mower/">riding mower rankings</a> start where this guide leaves off, and for very large, flat properties the <a href="/best-zero-turn-mower/">zero-turn guide</a> is the next step.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical mowing time by grass area (21-inch self-propelled)</p>
