        <div id="deck-and-cut" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Deck Width, Deck Material and Cut Quality
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most self-propelled mowers use a 21-inch deck because it balances coverage against maneuverability. A 22-inch deck adds a little coverage on each pass. A 30-inch deck adds a lot but changes the mower into a much heavier machine. For most yards, deck width should be the last thing you upgrade, after drive type and power source.
          </p>

          <p>
            Deck material affects lifespan more than cut. Stamped steel is common and fine as long as you scrape off wet clippings and keep it dry; neglected steel decks rust through at the seams after several seasons. Cast aluminum decks, found on premium models, do not rust and tend to outlast the engine. Polymer and composite decks on battery mowers also resist rust and keep weight down.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cut quality comes mostly from blade sharpness, blade speed and deck shape. A deck designed for mulching keeps clippings circulating so the blade can chop them several times before they drop. Dual-blade and stacked-blade designs give a cleaner finish and finer mulch at the cost of a slightly harder pull on the engine or battery. A dull blade will tear grass regardless of the deck, leaving white, frayed tips that brown within a day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Look for a 3-in-1 deck if you want flexibility: bag in spring and fall, mulch in summer, side-discharge when the grass gets away from you. Single-point or two-point height adjustment is a convenience worth paying a little extra for, because it encourages you to raise the deck in hot weather, which is one of the easiest ways to keep a lawn healthy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="storage-weight" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Storage, Weight and Handling
          </h2>

          <p>
            A self-propelled mower is heavier than a push mower because it carries a transmission or drive motor, and that weight is noticeable when you lift it into a truck, carry it up steps or wrestle it around a tight shed. Check the listed weight before you buy, especially if the mower lives in a basement or has to climb a few steps to reach the lawn.
          </p>

          <p>
            Storage footprint is the hidden cost in small garages. Many newer mowers fold the handle flat, and some can stand on end. Gas models that stand vertically need an engine designed for it so oil does not migrate into the cylinder or air filter. Battery mowers stand up or hang on a wall without that concern, which is one reason they suit apartments-with-a-yard and townhomes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Handle design matters more than people expect. A handle that adjusts to more than one height suits taller and shorter users in the same household. Drive bails that are easy to hold for an hour save sore hands. Owners commonly complain about drive levers that need a strong grip on budget models, so if you have arthritis or grip trouble, a pace-sensing handle is worth the upgrade.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wheel size changes handling on rough ground. Larger rear wheels roll over bumps and holes more easily and are less likely to scalp. Ball-bearing wheels last longer than bushings on plastic hubs. These details rarely show up in marketing, but they are the parts that wear out first on cheaper mowers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Keeping the Self-Propel Drive Working
          </h2>

          <p>
            The drive system is the part of a self-propelled mower that fails most often, and most of those failures are slow wear, not sudden breakage. On gas models a belt runs from the engine to a transmission, and a cable from the handle tightens that belt when you squeeze the bail. Over time the cable stretches and the belt glazes, and the mower starts to feel weak or slip under load.
          </p>

          <p>
            The first fix is almost always the cable adjuster. Most mowers have a barrel or a nut near the handle or the transmission that takes up slack. A few turns restore full engagement. If that does not help, look under the belt cover for a glazed, cracked or loose belt; belts are cheap and are a normal wear item every few seasons on a mower that sees regular use.
          </p>

          <p>
            Grass packing is the other common culprit. Clippings build up around the drive wheels, pinion gears and the transmission pulley and act like a brake. Pop the wheels off once a season, clear debris from the gear teeth and check that the small pawls inside each wheel move freely. Worn plastic wheel gears are a common, inexpensive replacement.
          </p>

          <p>
            Battery mowers skip belts and cables but still need the wheels and drive gears kept clean, and they need the battery stored indoors at a moderate charge over winter. Whatever the fuel, sharpen or replace the blade at least once a season, and if your mower stops driving entirely, check the simple items first before paying for a transmission. Our sister guide to <a href="/best-gas-lawn-mowers/">gas lawn mowers</a> covers the full seasonal engine routine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="clippings" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Bagging, Mulching and Side Discharge on a Self-Propelled Mower
          </h2>

          <p>
            How you handle clippings changes how the drive works. A rear bag adds weight behind the rear axle as it fills, often several pounds of wet grass by the end of a row. That extra weight helps a rear-drive mower grip and hurts a front-drive mower, which is why bagging owners on any kind of slope are steered toward rear-wheel drive.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mulching is the lowest-effort option for healthy, regularly mown turf. The clippings fall back as fine pieces that break down within days and return nitrogen to the soil. It only works well if you cut no more than about a third of the blade height at a time. Let the grass get long and mulching leaves wet clumps that smother the lawn and clog the deck.
          </p>

          <p>
            Side discharge is the escape hatch. When the lawn has run away after a rainy week or a vacation, pop in the side chute, raise the deck, and take a first pass to knock the height down. A second, lower pass a few days later with the mulch plug installed tidies things up. Trying to bag or mulch overgrown grass in one go is how drive belts get overworked.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bag capacity and the ease of emptying matter more than spec sheets admit. A bag with a rigid frame and a top handle is easier to lift off and dump than a floppy cloth sack. If you bag every week, check that the bag clips on without fighting the rear door, because you will do it dozens of times each season.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. What to Spend on a Self-Propelled Mower
          </h2>

          <p>
            Entry gas self-propelled mowers from mainstream brands usually run roughly $350 to $450 at the time of writing. That buys a 21-inch steel deck, front-wheel drive, often a single speed and a basic engine. For a flat lot and an owner who keeps up with oil and fuel stabilizer, it is enough mower.
          </p>

          <p>
            The mid-range, roughly $450 to $700, is where most buyers should land. Here you get rear-wheel drive, variable or pace-sensing speed, better handles and decks, and longer warranties. Battery kits in this range typically include one battery and a charger, and the better ones can mow a quarter acre or more per charge.
          </p>

          <p>
            Premium walk-behinds, roughly $700 to $1,500, add cast aluminum decks, all-wheel drive, wider decks or larger battery packs. They make sense if you mow a big or steep property, intend to keep the mower 10 years or more, or want a battery mower with enough capacity to never think about runtime. If you find yourself pricing a 30-inch walk-behind above $1,200, compare it against an entry riding mower before you commit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Budget for the extras too. A second battery, a bagger that is sold separately on some models, a spare blade and a season of oil and filters add up. Buying from a store with a local service counter or an authorized dealer is worth a modest premium, because warranty work on a mower usually means hauling it somewhere.
          </p>

