        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying front-wheel drive for a sloped yard</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Front-drive mowers lose traction uphill as weight shifts to the rear, and a filling grass bag makes it worse. If any part of your lawn climbs noticeably, buy rear-wheel or all-wheel drive. Owners who ignore this end up pushing a self-propelled mower up the same bank every week.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the box runtime on a battery mower</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Runtime figures assume ideal conditions. Long, damp or dense grass can cut real runtime significantly. Measure your actual grass area, compare it honestly to the rating, and plan on a second battery if you are near the limit. Running out halfway is the most common battery-mower complaint.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Never adjusting the drive cable</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A self-propelled drive that feels weak is usually a stretched cable, not a dead transmission. Most mowers have a simple adjuster that restores full engagement in a minute. Check it every spring before assuming something expensive has failed or that the mower is simply worn out.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mowing tall grass at full speed</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Pushing a self-propelled mower fast through overgrown grass gives a ragged cut, clogs the chute and bag, and drains batteries. Raise the deck, slow the drive and take narrower passes. You will finish sooner overall because you will not be stopping to clear clogs. Shorter strokes also keep the drive from straining.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying for self-propulsion you do not need</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                On a small, flat lawn of a few thousand square feet, a self-propelled drive adds weight, cost and parts that can fail. A light push mower or a compact battery mower will do the job well and be easier to store and carry. Put the savings toward a sharp spare blade instead.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

