        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which self-propelled mower suits most households?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a typical flat-to-rolling suburban yard, the Toro Recycler 22-inch Personal Pace is the easiest all-round recommendation because its speed-sensing drive is simple to use and its deck mulches well. If you want to leave gas behind, the EGO LM2135SP is the stronger battery choice, especially on slopes, thanks to rear-wheel drive and a capable 56-volt battery system.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is rear-wheel drive better than front-wheel drive?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Rear-wheel drive is better on slopes and when bagging, because weight shifts onto the rear wheels as you climb and as the bag fills, improving grip. Front-wheel drive is easier to turn because you can lift the drive wheels and pivot. On flat lots with lots of obstacles, front drive is fine; on any noticeable slope, choose rear drive.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a battery self-propelled mower handle half an acre?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Some can, but usually only with a large battery or a second pack. Box runtimes assume ideal conditions, and dense or damp grass shortens them. For half an acre of actual grass, either buy a kit with two batteries or choose a model with dual battery ports. Below a quarter acre, a single good battery typically finishes the job comfortably.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should a self-propelled mower last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A well-maintained gas self-propelled mower from a mainstream brand commonly lasts eight to ten years, and premium models with aluminum decks often go longer. The drive belt and cable are normal wear parts that may need replacing every few seasons. Battery mowers have fewer wear parts, though the battery itself will gradually lose capacity and may need replacing after several years.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my self-propelled mower feel weak going uphill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The usual causes are a stretched drive cable, a worn or glazed belt, grass packed around the wheel gears, or simply front-wheel drive losing traction as weight shifts backward. Start by adjusting the cable, then clean the wheels and check the belt. If the mower is front-wheel drive and the slope is steep, the layout itself may be the limit.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is self-propulsion worth it on a small lawn?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not always. On a small, flat lawn, a lightweight push mower or a compact battery mower is easier to store, cheaper and has fewer parts to fail. Self-propulsion earns its keep when the yard has slopes, when you bag clippings regularly, or when the mowing takes long enough that pushing becomes tiring.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a 30-inch walk-behind better than a riding mower?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on access and terrain. A 30-inch walk-behind fits through standard gates, handles terraces and steep banks that are unsafe for riders, and takes less storage space. A riding mower is faster and less tiring on large, open, mostly level lawns. If the property is over three quarters of an acre of open grass, a rider is usually the better value.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
